# {{ projectName }}

Built with the **slim** starter kit — the smallest Lockness project that does
something. One controller, one named middleware, and nothing you did not ask
for.

## Run it

```bash
deno task dev      # http://localhost:8888, reloads on change
deno task test     # the smoke test
```

`GET /` returns JSON. `GET /hello` returns JSON through a named middleware.

## What is here

```text
app/
├── controller/app_controller.ts    # two routes
├── middleware/example_middleware.ts # @Middleware('example')
├── kernel.ts                        # the whole configuration
└── routes.ts                        # explicit registry, for production
config/
├── app.ts        # name, env, debug, url
├── compile.ts    # deno task compile
├── listeners.ts  # event listeners
└── mod.ts        # the central export
main.ts           # createApp(AppKernel)
cli.ts            # deno task cli
tests/            # the smoke test
```

Two packages: `@lockness/core` and `@lockness/cli`. That is the entire
dependency list.

## What is deliberately missing

No authentication, no session, no database, no view layer, no Tailwind. This
kit is the clean slate — you add a package when you need it rather than
deleting six you do not.

If you want those wired up already, scaffold a different kit instead:

```bash
deno run -A jsr:@lockness/init my-app --kit=web   # JSX, Tailwind, auth, Drizzle
deno run -A jsr:@lockness/init my-app --kit=api   # JSON API, tokens, OpenAPI
```

## Adding a package

Each one is the same three steps. Sessions, for example:

```bash
deno add jsr:@lockness/session
```

```ts
// config/session.ts
import type { SessionConfig } from '@lockness/core'

export const sessionConfig: SessionConfig = {
    driver: 'cookie',
    secret: Deno.env.get('APP_KEY'),
    lifetime: 7200,
    secure: Deno.env.get('APP_ENV') === 'production',
}
```

```ts
// config/mod.ts — add it to the object
import { sessionConfig } from './session.ts'
export const config = { app: appConfig, session: sessionConfig, /* … */ } as const

// app/kernel.ts — name it
@Kernel({ session: config.session })
```

The same shape works for `@lockness/drizzle` (`database`), `@lockness/cache`
(`cache`), and `@lockness/devtools` (`devtools: true`).

## Views

The slim kit has no renderer, no layouts and no Tailwind. Adding them takes
nothing but a rename: JSX ships inside `@lockness/core`, and `deno.json`
already carries the two compiler options it needs.

```tsx
// app/controller/app_controller.tsx  ← renamed from .ts
@Get('/', { name: 'home' })
index(c: Context) {
    return c.render(<h1>Hello</h1>)
}
```

> **Do not delete `compilerOptions` from `deno.json`** on the grounds that this
> kit has no views. `@lockness/core` renders its own 404 and 500 pages in JSX,
> and Deno applies your project's compiler options to every module it loads,
> the framework's included. Without them `deno check` fails with 149 errors
> inside a package you never edited.

## Commands

```bash
deno task cli make:controller Product   # scaffold a controller
deno task cli make:middleware Auth      # scaffold a middleware
deno task cli router:list               # every registered route
deno task routes:generate               # refresh app/routes.ts
```

